 \subsection{Contributions and imported proofs}\label{sec:provenance}
 
-Troy Nguyen, of Bothell, Washington, elicited the discovery from GPT~6.1~Sol (OpenAI) through a sustained investigation of consequences of the ETH refutations. Nguyen set the objective of identifying a contrary algorithm for a major complexity conjecture and directed the search toward a complete formal verification. GPT~6.1~Sol developed the bounded-degree, domain-uniform CSP argument, the sparse-input and native-runtime transfers, the graph and treewidth connection, and the downstream Lean development and exposition.
+Troy Nguyen, of Bothell, Washington, initiated and directed an AI-assisted investigation of consequences of the recent ETH refutations, with a focus on binary CSP and formal verification. Nguyen selected the research objectives and directed the development. GPT~6.1~Sol developed the bounded-degree, domain-uniform CSP argument, the sparse-input and native-runtime transfers, the graph and treewidth connection, and the downstream Lean development and exposition.
 
 The quantitative counting recursion, clique-level bounds, grouping routine, and native simulation infrastructure are reused from Claude (Anthropic)~\cite{ClaudeETH,AnthropicLean}. The spectral expander construction and weighted-tree foundations are reused from the OpenAI mathematics repository~\cite{OpenAIGraph}. The original source attributions are retained above the imported graph blocks, and a manifest records their copied ranges and compatibility changes. These imports are algorithmic and proof dependencies of the result, rather than claims of independent rediscovery.
 
